WEEK 9 PROJECT REPORT

Chicken Cross Road Game

1. Introduction

In Week 9, the main focus was to improve the user interface and add data persistence to the Chicken Cross Road game. A main menu, game over screen, restart and exit options were added. A high-score system was also implemented using file handling.

2. Objectives

The main objectives of Week 9 were:

To create a user-friendly main menu.

To display the player's score and high score.

To create a game over screen.

To add Restart and Exit buttons.

To save the player's high score in a file.

To load the saved high score when the game starts again.

To organize the user interface using a separate UIManager class.


3. Main Menu

A main menu was created for the game. It appears when the game starts.

The main menu contains:

CHICKEN CROSS ROAD

[ START GAME ]

[ EXIT ]

Use W A S D to move

The Start Game button starts the gameplay, while the Exit button closes the application.

4. Score Display

A score display was added to the gameplay screen.

It shows:

Score: 5
High Score: 10

The score increases when the chicken successfully moves forward.

5. Game Over Screen

When the chicken collides with a car or falls into the river, the game enters the Game Over state.

The Game Over screen displays the current score and high score.

It also provides two options:

Restart

Exit


6. Restart Function

The Restart button allows the player to start the game again after losing.

When Restart is selected:

The chicken returns to its starting position.

The current score is reset to zero.

Game objects are recreated.

The game returns to the Playing state.


7. Exit Function

An Exit button was implemented in both the Main Menu and Game Over screen.

When the player selects Exit, the game application closes.

8. UIManager.java

A new class named UIManager.java was created to manage the game's user interface.

It manages three different states:

MAIN_MENU
PLAYING
GAME_OVER

The class is responsible for drawing the main menu, score display, game over screen, and buttons.

9. SaveSystem.java

A new class named SaveSystem.java was created for high-score data persistence.

It contains two important methods:

saveHighScore()

and

loadHighScore()

The saveHighScore() method stores the high score in a file, while loadHighScore() reads the saved high score when the game starts.

10. Data Persistence

Data persistence allows important game data to remain available after the game is closed.

In this project, the high score is stored in:

highscore.txt

For example:

Game Score = 10
       ↓
New High Score
       ↓
Save to File
       ↓
Game Closed
       ↓
Game Started Again
       ↓
High Score = 10

Therefore, the high score is not lost when the game is restarted.

